[2026-05-23T02:14:25.780Z] Final output
# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: working tree diff
Verdict: needs-attention

No-ship: D1 works only as a normal ignore hint, while D3 is still bypassable and D2 verification is not executable as written.

Findings:
- [high] Pre-commit validates the working tree, not the staged commit (tools/install-hooks.sh:29-43)
  The generated hook only runs `bash "$SCRIPT"`, and the script reads files from disk. A user can stage a forbidden `research/` reference or bad `research/*.md`, then edit the working tree back to a clean state without staging; the hook passes while the index still commits the violation. This is a real enforcement gap without using `--no-verify`.
  Recommendation: Make the hook validate the staged snapshot: enumerate `git diff --cached --name-only -z`, read staged content with `git show :path`, and fail on violations in the index rather than the working tree.
- [high] Research reference detector misses common direct links from nested notes (tools/validate-research-isolation.sh:54-60)
  The detector only matches `](research/`, `](../research/`, and quoted `research/...`. Most notes under `concepts/<topic>/...` or `models/<MODEL>/...` would naturally link to the root research tree as `../../research/...`, or use bare/angle/reference-style Markdown links. Those direct citations are prohibited by policy but pass the current regex, so D3 does not actually enforce isolation for realistic paths.
  Recommendation: Detect any Markdown/path reference that resolves to repo-root `research/`, including `./research`, `../*/research`, bare paths, angle links, and reference definitions; preferably normalize relative links against each file path before deciding.
- [medium] Raw ignore rule is not an enforcement boundary for the 16GB import (.gitignore:34-35)
  `models/*/raw/source_code/` and `models/*/raw/manuals/` are ignored by normal `git add .`, but `.gitignore` does not stop `git add -f` or already-tracked paths. There is no index check in the hook rejecting staged raw files, so the 16GB testsuite can still enter a commit by accident during the migration commit phase.
  Recommendation: Add a pre-commit staged-path guard that rejects `models/<model>/raw/source_code/**` and `models/<model>/raw/manuals/**`, plus an optional large-file threshold check for staged blobs.
- [medium] Checksum verify plan compares manifests that will not diff cleanly (plan.md:391-410)
  Phase 1 writes source manifests from `E:\...` trees, while Phase 3 says to generate target manifests and `diff` them. Unless paths are normalized, otherwise identical files will still differ because the manifest filenames contain different source and target prefixes. That breaks the claimed copy-verify gate and invites manual override during a high-volume migration.
  Recommendation: Generate checksum manifests with relative paths from each tree root, for example `cd "$root" && find . -type f -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum`, then diff only normalized relative-path manifests.

Next steps:
- Fix staged-content validation before relying on the hook.
- Broaden research reference detection with path normalization tests.
- Add an index guard for ignored raw trees.
- Normalize migration checksum manifests before starting Phase 1.
